A||||10||Swany||temp gauge/ but not overheating?||||1||
Z||000000||Swany||08-15-2001||10:34 AM||||Hey guys, <P>ran into a little trouble this morning on my way to work. but first a quick history.<P>over the past couple of days my temp gauge would randomly rise up to the red, then quickly drop back into normal ranges. Over a time of about a minute. I was thinking thats pretty weird, but not too worried about it. <P>Then this morning, on my 3 mile drive to work, the temp gauge rises again, this time it pegs itself. the car has only be running for 5 mins. and is completely normal. <P>then the temp light comes on. Now I notice something that looks like a puff of smoke/steam/dust that came out of the vents ontop of the dash. not constant, just a puff. Practically at work, I continue on. and then notice a feeling like something is hitting the bottom of the floor boards below my feet. <P>after parking the car. I get out and pop the front hood. the fan on the radiator is still spinning down, and there doesn't seem to be any unusual heat, no steam, etc. <P>I take a quick look under the car and notice something leaking from the front right side. behing the front right tire.<P>Being in a hurry, wasn't able to check it out any further.<P>Oh, totally forgot, I've got a 86 GT that is stock. I've only had it since may, and haven't had a problem like this in the past. <P>Sorry this was so long, <P>So, any ideas? broken hose maybe? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-15-2001||10:51 AM||||How about your coolant level?<BR>Is the over flow bottle full and over flowing?<BR>Raise the front of the car to find your leak, might be a hose, a loose clamp or the coolant line has a hole in it.<P>Banging in the floor board?<BR>Look for anything that is loose or hanging.<P>Puff of smoke out of vents on dash.<P>There is a drain hole in the heater core box. If you get any smoke from the dash it comes from the heater core.<BR>This too may be bad.<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||California Kid||08-15-2001||10:54 AM||||Sounds like the heater core blew on you. If the water isn't coming from the overflow bottle, Don't drive it or you're going to burn the engine up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Swany||08-15-2001||12:21 PM||||After a quick look on a break, the coolant bottle is bone dry.. wasn't able to get the radiator cap of yet. wasn't hot, just was being a pain to get off. <P>So the coolant bottle isn't overflowing, its dry. <P>I'm starting to think heater core maybe too. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||California Kid||08-15-2001||01:18 PM||||There's no way that the coolant overflow bottle should be dry, unless it has a hole in the bottom of it!!!! There should always be fluid in it up to the hot or cold line molded into the bottle.<P>Almost sounds like you let the fluid get too low and the rest evaporated over time. Whatever, you now have air trapped inside your cooling system and if the air pocket gets to the water pump location it will cause the pump to cavitate, meaning sometimes it won't pump fluid resulting in erratic temperature readings. If you don't already own a "Chilton" Service manual for your car, get one from local auto parts, tells you how to properly fill cooling system, which can be tricky.||||reg||5
Z||000005||Swany||08-15-2001||02:25 PM||||well I know it had fluid in it in the past, I remember checking it. but when I went to check it on my last break it was dry. <P>being here in AZ, it could have emptied as recently as this morning and be dry a few hours later when I checked it. <P>Also, right after I parked it was draining out the ac drain. that has me worried. <P>but I'm not sure.. planning on pulling it in the back here and having a better look at it this afternoon. <P>Hopefully it won't be too bad||||reg||1
Z||000006||ka4nkf||08-15-2001||10:52 PM||||Use a small oil-filter wrench to get those radiator caps off. check your thermostat and make sure it is not close shut. It sounds like you have air in the system..||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-15-2001||11:25 PM||||Coolant? evaporate from the recovery tank? Coolant bottle don't work that way.<P>Coolant bottles get sucked dry. Happens allot. all it needs is a small leak in the system.<P>1. system heats up<BR>2. heat pushes out coolant from cap and leak.<BR>3. system cools down and sucks coolant back in from tank. Leak is too small right now to let in allot of air.<BR>4. since more coolant went out of the system than what went into the recovery tank, the level in the tank starts dropping. <BR>5. After however many heat/cool cycles the recovery tank is dry and the system sucks air.<BR>6. the engine, being a high point in the loop, catches the air and eventually over heats.<P>this can take a few days or several weeks or even longer. it all depends on the size/location of the leak. <P>Hose joint leaks are especially good for this. The pressure pushes coolant out but the rubber seals enough to keep air out when it cools. The cap on the engine or radiator can do it too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||getterw||08-16-2001||12:08 AM||||The swinging of the temp gage was caused by air in the cooling system.  <P>The puffs of steam and the drip near the right front tire is coolant leaking from your heater core.  It ruptured when the car over heated.  The drip is coming from the heater core drain tube just inboard and aft of the right front tire.  <P>The overflow tank is dry because you're losing coolant and what's left in the tank is being drawn into he radiator when the system cools.  <P>You might have another coolant leak (besides the heater core) which caused the low coolant and let air get into the system in the first place.<P>A new heater core will solve that part of the problem. Then you'll want to check the whole cooling system for leaks and be sure all the air is out of the system when you refill it. <P>To refill, jack up the rear of the vehicle, remove the thermostat and fill through the thermostat housing on the engine until coolant comes out of the radiator openning.  Put the cap on and lower the vehicle.  Finish filing at the thermostat housing.  When it looks full.  Put the thermostat cap back on (but leave the themostat out for the time being).  Run the car for about 15-20 minutes and watch the temp gauge.  Let the car cool off and remove thermostat cap and file more as need.  Repeat the run and fill cycle until all the air is out of the system.  Put the themostat back in and replace the cap.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Swany||08-16-2001||11:48 AM||||well, after bringing the overflow tank up to the right level, and checking the main water level, that was full. I was starting to think its not the heater core after all, because the radiator still had water in it. <P>We swapped out the thermostat, and that made all the difference. It never got about 180, the car has never ran that cool since I got it, so the thermostat was probally going bad for a little while. <P>Thanks for all your help, and I'm glad it was something as simple and cheap as a thermostat. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Swany||08-31-2001||06:48 PM||||UPDATE<P>Well, you guys were right, as you all normally are, :-)<P>Today the heater core finally gave out, drained water all over the inside of my car. <P>I spent the afternoon working on it, and I'm happy to say, my Fiero is back on the road. <P>I ended up just bypassing it. I live in Phoenix, so having no heat isn't the end of the world. I figure I can live without. <P>Thanks again for you help.. :-)||||reg||10
